# Spec-Driven Development

`AGENTS.md` is the canonical contract for this repo. Read it before non-trivial work.

## Flow (never skip ahead)

```
/speckit.constitution → /speckit.specify → /speckit.clarify → /speckit.plan
                      → /speckit.tasks   → /speckit.analyze  → /speckit.implement
```

In Cursor these are installed as skills named `speckit-<name>` under `.cursor/skills/`.

- One feature = one branch = one `specs/<n>-<slug>/` directory.
- `spec.md` = what & why. `plan.md` = how. Don't mix them.
- `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` blocks planning — resolve first.
- No implementation code before `tasks.md` exists.
- `/speckit.converge` adopts existing code into the flow.

## Token discipline

- Prefer [RTK](https://github.com/rtk-ai/rtk) for shell work: `rtk ls`, `rtk read`,
  `rtk grep`, `rtk find`, `rtk git status`, `rtk git diff`, `rtk test`, `rtk lint`,
  `rtk err`. Same information, 60–90% fewer tokens. `rtk gain` shows the savings.
- Keep prose dense: no preamble, no recap, no filler. Code and paths stay verbatim.
- Read the smallest slice of a file that answers the question.

## Secrets

Never hardcode or commit API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, or `.env` files.
`.env.example` holds key names with dummy values. Never print a secret's value.

## Verification and commits

- Done = a real command passed and you read its output.
- New behavior ships with a test that fails without the change.
- [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type(scope): subject`.
- Don't commit, push, or open PRs unless asked.

